[approval] bash: # Let me understand the graph structure better
# In a de Bruijn graph for genome assembly:
# - Each k-mer is a node
# - A k-mer "ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACT" with right extension "G" means:
#   the next k-mer in the sequence is "TAGCCAGTGCCTGATACTG" (shift right, add G)
# - The left extension "F" means no k-mer extends this one on the left
# 
# So the graph is: each k-mer node has:
#   - forward edges based on right extension (the next base in the sequence)
#   - backward edges based on left extension (the previous base)
#
# To build a contig:
#   1. Find a source node (no incoming edges / left_ext == 'F')
#   2. Extend forward by following right extensions
#   3. The contig is built by concatenating the k-mer + each right extension base
#
# Let me trace through the first few lines:
# ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACT  FG  -> left=F(no left ext), right=G
# TAGCCAGTGCCTGATACTG  AG  -> left=A, right=G
# AGCCAGTGCCTGATACTGG  TC  -> left=T, right=C
# GCCAGTGCCTGATACTGGC  AT  -> left=A, right=T
# CCAGTGCCTGATACTGGCT  GG  -> left=G, right=G

# So ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACT has right_ext=G, meaning the next base is G
# TAGCCAGTGCCTGATACTG has left_ext=A, meaning the prev base was A
# These are consistent: ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACT + G = ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACTG
# and TAGCCAGTGCCTGATACTG starts with A (from ATAGCCAGTGCCTGATACT)

echo "Understanding confirmed" — approved
